description:
crates/outram-foam-basic-lib/src/fv_operators/fvm/laplacian.rs:38 documents:

    let eqn = fvm::ddt(&phi, &phi_old, dt) - fvm::laplacian(&gamma_f, &phi);

That sign is WRONG FOR THIS PORT and describes anti-diffusion, which would amplify a gradient instead of smoothing it. Found 2026-08-12 while verifying the axial conduction term in tampines-steam-tables, whose vendored copy of the same file carried the identical text and has been corrected there.

WHY IT IS WRONG HERE EVEN THOUGH IT IS RIGHT IN OPENFOAM. This is the subtle part and is why it survived. OpenFOAM's laplacianFoam genuinely writes 'solve(fvm::ddt(T) - fvm::laplacian(DT, T))', so the minus reads as canonical to anyone who knows OpenFOAM. But this port's matrix has the OPPOSITE sign convention:

    mat.ldu.diag[o] += coeff;
    mat.ldu.diag[n] += coeff;
    mat.ldu.upper[f] = -coeff;
    mat.ldu.lower[f] = -coeff;

Positive diagonal with negative off-diagonals is a POSITIVE-DEFINITE operator, i.e. it represents -div(gamma grad phi), the negation of OpenFOAM's fvm::laplacian. So the heat equation

    d(phi)/dt - div(gamma grad phi) = 0

assembles here as ddt + laplacian, not ddt - laplacian.

EVIDENCE THE CODE AGREES: every real caller already uses +. In tampines-steam-tables's rhoPimpleFoam the momentum predictor is '... + fvm::laplacian_vec(&mu, &u, ...)' (mod.rs:1352) and the energy equation uses '+ fvm::laplacian(&alpha_h_f, &he)'. Note that OpenFOAM's own UEqn uses MINUS in the same place, which confirms the port negates rather than that the callers are wrong.

VERIFIED IDENTICAL: the laplacian assembly in crates/outram-foam-basic-lib/src/fv_operators/fvm/laplacian.rs and the tampines-steam-tables vendored copy match line for line in sign, so the correction that was applied to the vendored copy applies verbatim here.

SCOPE: documentation only -- the implementation is correct and no caller is affected. But this is a doc that will actively mislead someone writing a new equation, and it is more dangerous than an ordinary doc error precisely because it matches the OpenFOAM idiom a reader is likely to arrive with. Check whether other fvm operator docs in the same directory carry the same inherited sign assumption.

Also worth checking: any other crate vendoring a copy of this file.
